JSON implementation alternatives benchmark
Date: 2026-10-17
Command: python scripts/benchmark_json_paths.py --loops 1500 --repeats 7 --output docs/provider_verification/json-path-benchmark.json
Environment: CPython 3.13.5; orjson 3.13.0; pydantic 2.14.1;
             pydantic-core 2.50.1; SQLAlchemy 2.1.5
Payloads: actual OperationRecord with 48 nested checkpoint items, MemoryEvent,
          MemoryRecord -> AssertionEnvelope via to_envelope
Warm-up: 1 call per variant; reported metric is median across 7 repeats

OperationRecord JSON-mode validation (ops/s; larger typed payload)
  json.dumps + model_validate_json:                    12,285.2
  compact json.dumps + model_validate_json:            12,130.8
  orjson.dumps + model_validate_json:                  16,186.7
  recursive compatibility guard + orjson/fallback:      7,822.5
  model_validate(python-mode model_dump):              19,597.4
  model_dump_json:                                      69,906.3
  model_validate_json(model_dump_json):                 13,031.3
`model_validate(python-mode model_dump)` is not a replacement for DB row
validation: direct model_validate on the actual JSON-mode DB mapping failed
strict enum and timestamp checks. The model_dump_json figures benchmark another
input shape and do not establish interchangeable persisted bytes.

Actual edge cases in arbitrary OperationCheckpoint.details
  NaN / +Inf / -Inf:
    stdlib json dumps + JSON-mode validation preserved the non-finite float;
    orjson serialized it as null and validation produced None.
  Integer 2**80:
    stdlib round trip preserved the exact integer; orjson encoding raised
    TypeError due to its default 64-bit integer bound.
  Integer dictionary key:
    stdlib JSON coercion preserved prior JSON behavior by turning it into "7";
    orjson raised TypeError without a non-string-key option.
  Lone surrogate:
    stdlib encoded an escape but the Pydantic JSON parser rejected it;
    orjson raised TypeError earlier.
  Datetime object in unknown details:
    stdlib raised TypeError; orjson silently emitted an ISO string.
  Custom object:
    both encoders raised TypeError.
The recursive guard retained these distinctions but cost more than the stdlib
path on the ordinary payload. No provider source changes were adopted.

Canonical event payload
  Existing orjson canonical_payload:                    4,184,099.8 ops/s
  stdlib sorted/compact UTF-8, allow_nan=False:            336,413.4 ops/s
  Bytes and xxhash fingerprint matched for the sampled payload when stdlib was
  explicitly configured for UTF-8 and compact sorted output. Default ASCII
  escaping changed the sample hash. NaN, infinities, and >64-bit integers also
  have different or incompatible canonical outputs; the production encoder is
  unchanged.

Envelope serialization
  AssertionEnvelope.model_dump_json:                    130,630.2 ops/s
  orjson(model_dump(mode="json")):                     127,311.8 ops/s
  compact UTF-8 stdlib(model_dump(mode="json")):        67,543.8 ops/s
  Parsed output values and record round trip matched. No envelope source change.

Decision
  Keep the existing orjson event fingerprint and selected store/API paths.
  Keep provider change JSON-mode validation using stdlib serialization because
  unconditional orjson changes unknown-extra values/errors and a compatibility
  tree walk erased the apparent ordinary-payload speedup. Keep envelope's
  Pydantic serializer; no measured gain justified replacing it.
  This is evidence for these sampled shapes and installed versions, not a
  universal JSON library ranking.
